## The conflict of interest, stated plainly

This site is published by Age Ayurveda, which sells Ayurvedic products at
<a href={STORE}>ageayurveda.com</a>. A company publishing a reference work about the category
it sells into has an obvious incentive problem. Here is how we handle it.

- **Reference pages are not sales pages.** No product is mentioned in the body of a monograph.
- **One disclosed block.** Where a page describes something we sell, a single labelled block
  at the end of the page says so and links to the product. It is visually separated and it
  says who publishes the site.
- **No page exists because a product needed one.** The monograph set was generated from a
  reference corpus covering the whole materia medica, not from our catalogue. Most pages have
  no commercial link at all.
- **No advertising, no sponsorship, no affiliate links.**

## How claims are worded

Ayurvedic products are regulated, and disease claims are a regulatory exposure rather than a
style preference. The rules we write to:

- In product blocks: "supports X function", or "traditionally used to support X". Never
  treats, cures, relieves or prevents. Never a disease name.
- In reference text: traditional indications are reported as the classical source states them,
  attributed to that source. Research findings are reported as the authors state them,
  attributed to the study. Neither is presented as advice, and neither appears next to a
  product link.
- Product names are never changed to make a claim easier to write. Some of our product names
  contain claim-like words. Renaming is a packaging and licensing decision, not a copy edit,
  so the name stays and the surrounding text does the work.

These rules are enforced mechanically, not by good intentions: the build fails if a disease
term appears inside a product block.

## What we will not publish

- Dosage recommendations for individuals, or anything that reads as a treatment plan.
- Manufacturing process detail. This is a reference for readers, not a formulation manual.
- Details of products that have not launched.
- Fabricated authorship or review credentials. See <a href={rel('/reviewers/')}>reviewers</a>
  for exactly where the Atlas stands on expert review today.

## Safety framing

Every monograph carries a safety and contraindications section where the source material has
one, and every page carries a disclaimer. Where a substance is restricted, toxic, or a
metallic or mineral preparation requiring specialist handling, we have kept it out of the
public Atlas entirely rather than publish a monograph a general reader might act on.

## Dates and revisions

Pages carry a last-reviewed date. Research sections state the year of the study. When we
correct something we say so on <a href={rel('/corrections/')}>corrections</a>.
